<Base title="Start Here · Capability Matters" active="start-here"
 description="Cases, experiments, and design briefs for people and teams engineering human capability. Explore independently or connect through Johns Hopkins LENS.">
  <link slot="head" rel="canonical" href="https://capabilitymatters.org/start-here/" />
  <header class="start-hero container-wide">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Start Here · Capability Matters</p>
      <h1>Turn a capability gap into a better design decision.</h1>
      <p class="lead">Explore how people learn, work, and perform inside complex systems—with cases to question, experiments to try, and prototypes to refine.</p>
      <p>Capability Matters connects learning engineering to what people need to do. Whether you lead a team, design a service, teach, research, or learn independently, start with a real problem and examine the evidence behind your next step.</p>
      <div class="hero-links"><a class="button" href="/learning-map/">Open the learning map</a><a href="#choose-a-path">Explore the site sections</a><a href="/">Read the motivation</a></div>
    </div>
    <aside aria-labelledby="question-heading" class="question-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">A question worth asking</p>
      <h2 id="question-heading">What should people be able to do—and under what conditions?</h2>
      <p>Define the capability. Examine the work system. Try a small change. Look for evidence of performance, learning, and transfer.</p>
      <p class="boundary">A polished output or a completed activity is a starting point for inquiry, not proof of capability.</p>
      <a href="/show-your-work/">Read Capability Matters</a>
    </aside>
  </header>

  <section class="container-wide" aria-label="Guided exploration"><h2>A little context before you jump in.</h2><p>The <a href="/learning-map/">learning map</a> connects the site’s cases, experiments, and prototypes. Each experiment and prototype brief has a short guide: prepare, try one task, notice what happened, and choose a next step.</p><p>Use your <a href="/learning-notebook/">reflection notebook</a> to connect experiences. Saving notes is optional and stays in your browser.</p></section>
  <section class="container-wide pathways" id="choose-a-path" aria-labelledby="paths-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Three ways in</p>
    <h2 id="paths-heading">Choose the work you want to do.</h2>
    <div class="path-grid">{paths.map(path => <article class="path-card">
      <span class="path-number" aria-hidden="true">{path.number}</span>
      <h3>{path.title}</h3><p>{path.text}</p><a href={path.url}><strong>{path.label}</strong> <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </article>)}</div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-wide audiences" aria-labelledby="audience-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">A shared practice · Different starting points</p>
    <h2 id="audience-heading">You do not need to be enrolled to begin.</h2>
    <div class="audience-grid">
      <article><h3>Independent practitioners &amp; teams</h3>
        <p>Use the public cases, labs, and design briefs to explore a question in your own setting. Leaders, educators, researchers, designers, and prospective partners can start here without a LENS affiliation.</p>
        <p>Bring a capability gap, compare possible approaches, and identify the next small test. These resources support inquiry; they do not promise a validated solution for your organization.</p>
        <a href="mailto:wgr@jhu.edu">Discuss a question or potential collaboration</a>
      </article>
      <article><h3>JHU LENS learners &amp; partners</h3>
        <p>The site also supports Johns Hopkins’ Learning Engineering for Next-Generation Systems (LENS) concentration. Connect the cases and experiments to its five competencies, or explore the program as a prospective learner or partner.</p>
        <p>The program is one pathway into this work. Enrollment is not a condition for exploring the public resources.</p>
        <a href="#people">Meet the people &amp; explore the program</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container context" aria-label="Context and resources">
    <p class="eyebrow">Context, people &amp; evidence</p>
    <h2>Read with the evidence in view.</h2>
    <p>Case studies document examples and their limitations. Labs and prototypes let you examine a mechanism or rehearse a decision; availability does not establish effectiveness. <a href="/show-your-work/">Capability Matters</a> is a LENS community paper on capability, coupled learner and system performance, and human flourishing.</p>
    <p>For the wider field’s reading lists, tools, events, and community, visit the
      <a href="https://lecommons.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Learning Engineering Commons</a>
      and <a href="https://sagroups.ieee.org/icicle/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEEE ICICLE</a>.</p>
    <h2 id="where-it-applies">Where it applies</h2>
    <p>
      LENS focuses on high-consequence, high-impact domains — where human capability is
      inseparable from system performance and the cost of getting it wrong is measured in
      lives, readiness, and equity. Seven domains recur across the program's case material and
      teaching: healthcare, defense &amp; national security, education at scale, aviation,
      energy, manufacturing &amp; industrial, and government &amp; public infrastructure.
    </p>

    <h2 id="framework">The LENS framework</h2>
    <p>
      LENS reads every case through five competencies. Each case study on this site is
      tagged to the competencies it exercises, so the same framework runs from a hospital to a
      flight deck to a public-sector IT migration:
    </p>
    <ol class="framework">
      <li><details><summary><strong>Systems Analysis</strong></summary><p>Represent people, tools, requirements, interfaces and feedback to locate a capability gap in its operating system.</p></details></li>
      <li><details><summary><strong>Iterative Development</strong></summary><p>Frame a problem, test a bounded change, examine the evidence and revisit the next decision. Refine throughout the cycle.</p></details></li>
      <li><details><summary><strong>Human-System Collaboration</strong></summary><p>Design roles, authority, coordination and opportunities for people to recognize, question and intervene in system behavior.</p></details></li>
      <li><details><summary><strong>Data, Measurement and Evaluation</strong></summary><p>Choose observations and instruments that can test a capability claim, and explain uncertainty and limits on the inference.</p></details></li>
      <li><details><summary><strong>Navigating Sociotechnical Constraints</strong></summary><p>Account for institutional rules, incentives, resources, access and governance when deciding what can be built and sustained.</p></details></li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="people">People &amp; programs</h2>
    <p>
      LENS is a concentration within the <a href="https://education.jhu.edu/masters-programs/master-of-education-in-learning-design-and-technology/learning-engineering-next-generation-systems-concentration/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JHU MEd in Learning Design and Technology</a>,
      jointly run by the Johns Hopkins School of Education and the Applied Physics Laboratory,
      with an IEEE ICICLE partnership. Faculty in the concentration include James Diamond (School of Education) and
      William Gray-Roncal (School of Education / APL). Full profiles for practitioners across the
      wider field live at <a href="https://lecommons.org/community/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">lecommons's community pages</a>.
    </p>

    <p>For Will Gray-Roncal’s other writing and projects, visit <a href="https://will.grayroncal.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">his personal site</a>.</p>

    <h2>Scope &amp; caveats</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A focused collection, not a complete index.</strong> These resources grew from
        LENS teaching and practice, and are available to independent visitors too. For the
        wider field, see the <a href="https://lecommons.org/">Learning Engineering Commons</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="https://sagroups.ieee.org/icicle/">IEEE ICICLE</a> is authoritative.</strong> When ICICLE and this site differ on a
        definition or taxonomy, defer to ICICLE. This site doesn't claim to speak for IEEE
        ICICLE or host its content — it connects to it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Links may rot.</strong> External resources change. <a href="https://github.com/wrgr/capabilitymatters/issues/new">Report a broken link</a> with the page address and the link you tried.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tools used</h2>
    <p>
      Claude (Anthropic) was used as an assistant throughout development and content
      curation — for code generation, draft copy, and triage of the underlying material.
      Final editorial decisions, scope, and accuracy review are human-authored.
    </p>

    <h2>Contributing</h2>
    <p>
      Suggestions and corrections are welcome. Visit the <a href="https://github.com/wrgr/capabilitymatters">source repository</a>, open an <a href="https://github.com/wrgr/capabilitymatters/issues">issue</a> or <a href="mailto:wgr@jhu.edu">contact Will Gray-Roncal</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

